Android link evidence for the seam (host, no device).

Toolchain: NDK 26.1.10909125, target aarch64-linux-android, API 26,
CC/CARGO_TARGET_AARCH64_LINUX_ANDROID_LINKER = aarch64-linux-android26-clang.

1) The crate itself builds for Android with the seam compiled in:

   $ cargo build --locked --lib --no-default-features \
         --features android-hardened --target aarch64-linux-android
       Finished `dev` profile [unoptimized + debuginfo] target(s) in 4m 03s

2) The released .so carries no reference to android_setsocknetwork yet, and that
   is expected at this milestone:

   $ llvm-readelf -d libtollgate_client_core.so | grep NEEDED
     NEEDED  libdl.so
     NEEDED  libc.so
   $ llvm-readelf --dyn-syms libtollgate_client_core.so | grep setsocknetwork
     (no match)

   Reason: nothing on the cdylib's exported surface reaches AndroidNetworkBinder
   yet -- the Java-side Network capture is the follow-up card -- so rustc drops
   the extern block as unreachable and never emits -landroid. Forcing the
   reference with -C link-arg=-Wl,--undefined=android_setsocknetwork also does not
   change DT_NEEDED, because the reference is optimised out before the linker
   sees it.

3) So the attribute and the sysroot were proved with a throwaway probe crate that
   references the symbol from main:

   $ cargo build --target aarch64-linux-android       # probe crate
       Finished `dev` profile [unoptimized + debuginfo] target(s) in 0.26s
   $ llvm-readelf -d target/aarch64-linux-android/debug/android-link-probe \
         | grep NEEDED
     NEEDED  libandroid.so
     NEEDED  libdl.so
     NEEDED  libc.so
   $ llvm-readelf --dyn-syms ... | grep setsocknetwork
     4: 0000000000000000 0 FUNC GLOBAL DEFAULT UND android_setsocknetwork

   => `#[link(name = "android")]` resolves against the NDK sysroot and lands
      libandroid.so in DT_NEEDED. The follow-up card only has to make the symbol
      reachable from the exported shell surface.

What this does NOT prove: that android_setsocknetwork attaches a socket to a real
Network on a device. That needs a device run with ConnectivityManager in hand.
